   "**When courts and conflict occupy the same calendar, the faster process sets the precedent** — the effect of a Hague judgment, an ICC appearance and an Amnesty finding inside 48 hours — all concerning conduct months or years old — is that the vocabulary of accountability grows sharper while its reach stays where it was; the compound consequence is that the language of war crimes becomes routinely applied to the past and studiously avoided about the present, which teaches every government that the proceedings are survivable",
   "**And an accountability that runs only one way becomes a weapon rather than a standard** — the effect of visa restrictions announced by Washington against officials of a state accused of discriminating against a white minority, framed as the first of \"escalating measures\", is that the same word — accountability — is now used both for a court that convicts a liberator and for a policy that penalises the country he was independent from; the compound consequence is that the currency of the word is debased in precisely the decade it is most needed, and the states most likely to be its targets will discount it in advance",
   "**But none of this is beyond correction, and every correction is procedural rather than heroic** — the moment evidence sites are protected and clearing is sequenced so that remains are identified before the rubble is lifted, the moment heavy machinery enters under humanitarian rather than security review, the moment investigators are admitted to all parts of the territory, the moment courts are allowed to finish the cases they have started without the verdict being pre-argued in the streets by either side, and the moment the standard applied abroad is the standard applied at home, the effect changes from accountability as an argument toward accountability as a record"
